OpenBSD kills a process whose stack pointer is outside a MAP_STACK mapping
at a syscall or page fault.  pthread_attr_setstack() on OpenBSD writes every
page of the supplied range and re-mmaps it MAP_STACK, which faults on Wine's
read-only kernel stack guard page.  Secondary threads therefore run on a
libpthread-allocated stack, recorded as kernel_stack at thread start with
pthread_stackseg_np(); only the initial thread gets a Wine kernel stack.
(Rebased for Proton's wine 11.0 layout; emulators/wine does this in
server_init_thread() for 11.19.)

